INTENT
Provide a public JSON HTTP API that turns a caller-supplied public http(s) URL into an unguessable short code, redirects visitors from that code to the original URL until the link expires, and exposes per-link click analytics that contain no visitor IP addresses.

DESIGN
{
  "configuration": [
    {
      "default": "./links.db",
      "name": "LINKS_DB_PATH",
      "purpose": "Filesystem path to the SQLite database file. This exact name is the single source of truth for both application code and tests; no alias such as DATABASE_PATH is read."
    },
    {
      "default": "http://localhost:8000",
      "name": "LINKS_BASE_URL",
      "purpose": "Origin used to build the short_url field in responses. Trailing slashes are stripped; short_url is f\"{LINKS_BASE_URL.rstrip('/')}/{code}\"."
    },
    {
      "default": "30",
      "name": "LINKS_DEFAULT_TTL_DAYS",
      "purpose": "Days added to created_at to compute expires_at when the request omits expires_at. Integer >= 1."
    },
    {
      "default": "2048",
      "name": "LINKS_MAX_URL_LENGTH",
      "purpose": "Maximum accepted length in characters of the url field; longer input is rejected with 422 validation_error."
    },
    {
      "default": "7",
      "name": "LINKS_CODE_LENGTH",
      "purpose": "Number of base62 characters in a generated short code. Also drives the redirect route's code pattern check."
    },
    {
      "default": "5",
      "name": "LINKS_CODE_MAX_ATTEMPTS",
      "purpose": "Bounded number of insert attempts when a generated code collides with the UNIQUE constraint before the request fails with 503 code_generation_failed."
    },
    {
      "default": "10",
      "name": "LINKS_RATE_LIMIT_MAX",
      "purpose": "Maximum POST /api/links creations allowed per client address per rolling window."
    },
    {
      "default": "60",
      "name": "LINKS_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS",
      "purpose": "Length in seconds of the rolling rate-limit window."
    },
    {
      "default": "true",
      "name": "LINKS_RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED",
      "purpose": "Set to \"false\" to disable the creation rate limiter (used by tests that create many links). Parsed case-insensitively; only \"false\"/\"0\"/\"no\" disable it."
    },
    {
      "default": "false",
      "name": "LINKS_TRUST_FORWARDED_FOR",
      "purpose": "When \"true\", the client address for rate limiting is the first entry of X-Forwarded-For; otherwise the peer address (request.client.host) is used. The value is never persisted or logged either way."
    },
    {
      "default": "50",
      "name": "LINKS_STATS_DEFAULT_LIMIT",
      "purpose": "Default number of click entries returned by the stats endpoint when limit is omitted."
    },
    {
      "default": "500",
      "name": "LINKS_STATS_MAX_LIMIT",
      "purpose": "Upper bound for the stats limit query parameter; a larger value is a 422 validation_error."
    },
    {
      "default": "true",
      "name": "LINKS_DNS_RESOLUTION_ENABLED",
      "purpose": "When \"true\", hostnames are resolved with the stdlib resolver and every returned address is denylist-checked. Tests stub the resolver rather than disabling this."
    },
    {
      "default": "INFO",
      "name": "LINKS_LOG_LEVEL",
      "purpose": "Root log level. Access logging is configured to omit client addresses at every level."
    }
  ],
  "data_model": [
    {
      "fields": [
        {
          "constraints": "PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT, not null. Internal only; never exposed in any response and never used to derive a code.",
          "name": "id",
          "type": "INTEGER"
        },
        {
          "constraints": "NOT NULL, UNIQUE. Exactly LINKS_CODE_LENGTH base62 characters. The UNIQUE constraint is the sole enforcement point for code uniqueness.",
          "name": "code",
          "type": "TEXT"
        },
        {
          "constraints": "NOT NULL, length 1..LINKS_MAX_URL_LENGTH, stored byte-for-byte as supplied including query string and fragment.",
          "name": "url",
          "type": "TEXT"
        },
        {
          "constraints": "NOT NULL. UTC ISO-8601 in the fixed format %Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S.%fZ so that lexicographic ordering equals chronological ordering.",
          "name": "created_at",
          "type": "TEXT"
        },
        {
          "constraints": "NOT NULL, same format as created_at, always strictly greater than created_at. Defaults to created_at + LINKS_DEFAULT_TTL_DAYS days when the request omits it.",
          "name": "expires_at",
          "type": "TEXT"
        }
      ],
      "indexes": [
        "UNIQUE index on links(code) (implicit from the UNIQUE constraint): serves GET /{code} and GET /api/links/{code}/stats with a single index lookup; without it both would full-scan the table on every visit."
      ],
      "name": "links",
      "purpose": "One row per shortened destination; the redirect hot path reads exactly one row from it by code."
    },
    {
      "fields": [
        {
          "constraints": "PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT, not null.",
          "name": "id",
          "type": "INTEGER"
        },
        {
          "constraints": "NOT NULL, FOREIGN KEY REFERENCES links(id) ON DELETE CASCADE. PRAGMA foreign_keys=ON at connection open.",
          "name": "link_id",
          "type": "INTEGER"
        },
        {
          "constraints": "NOT NULL. UTC ISO-8601, same fixed format as links.created_at; the request timestamp.",
          "name": "clicked_at",
          "type": "TEXT"
        },
        {
          "constraints": "NULLABLE. Verbatim Referer header, truncated to 2048 characters; NULL when the header is absent.",
          "name": "referrer",
          "type": "TEXT"
        },
        {
          "constraints": "NULLABLE. Verbatim User-Agent header, truncated to 2048 characters; NULL when the header is absent.",
          "name": "user_agent",
          "type": "TEXT"
        }
      ],
      "indexes": [
        "Index on clicks(link_id, clicked_at DESC, id DESC): serves both the paginated newest-first clicks list and the COUNT(*) for total_clicks in the stats endpoint. Without it every stats call scans the whole clicks table, which grows unbounded with traffic."
      ],
      "name": "clicks",
      "purpose": "Append-only redirect log. Deliberately has no column capable of holding a client IP address."
    }
  ],
  "decisions": [
    {
      "choice": "SQLite single file opened with PRAGMA journal_mode=WAL, PRAGMA synchronous=NORMAL, PRAGMA foreign_keys=ON, busy_timeout=5000ms; schema created idempotently at application startup via CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS.",
      "decision": "Storage engine and mode",
      "rationale": "Deployment is single node and the redirect path is one unique-index lookup plus one small insert; WAL lets the click insert proceed without blocking concurrent redirect reads, so a burst of visitors does not serialize behind each other's writes. busy_timeout turns a transient lock into a slightly slower redirect instead of a 500.",
      "rejected": [
        "Postgres or Redis: an external datastore is forbidden by the constraints and adds an operational dependency for a workload of one row read per click.",
        "Default rollback journal: writers block readers, so a click insert would stall concurrent redirects.",
        "Migration framework such as Alembic: for two tables created once at startup it is more machinery than the schema is worth."
      ]
    },
    {
      "choice": "Expired links return 410 Gone with {\"error\":{\"code\":\"link_expired\",...}}; unknown or malformed codes return 404 with {\"error\":{\"code\":\"not_found\",...}}. The two are deliberately distinguishable.",
      "decision": "What an expired code returns, and whether it is distinguishable from a code that never existed",
      "rationale": "A user who clicks a shared link needs to know the link was real and has lapsed (so they can ask for a fresh one) rather than that they mistyped. Codes are 62^7 random, so the existence oracle leaks nothing an attacker could enumerate; the acceptance criteria also pin 410 for expired and 404 for unknown. Expired links are retained rather than deleted so their analytics remain readable at /stats.",
      "rejected": [
        "Returning 404 for both: hides the difference between a lapsed link and a typo and would fail the pinned 410 criterion.",
        "Deleting rows at expiry: destroys the accumulated click history that the stats endpoint must still serve for expired links.",
        "Returning 410 with a Location header: some clients would follow it, defeating revocation."
      ]
    },
    {
      "choice": "Synchronously, inside the request handler, before the 307 response is returned: read the link, evaluate expiry, INSERT the click row and commit, then send the response.",
      "decision": "Where the click analytics write happens relative to the redirect response",
      "rationale": "The criteria require exactly one click row per successful redirect and total_clicks to equal successful redirects; a background task or buffered flush makes the stats endpoint return a stale count immediately after a click, which a user watching their dashboard would see as a lost click. The insert is a single append into a WAL-mode file, on the order of tens of microseconds, which is invisible next to the visitor's DNS+TCP to the destination.",
      "rejected": [
        "FastAPI BackgroundTasks or a queue flusher: a crash or a race between the redirect and a stats read loses or delays clicks, and tests asserting exactly one row become timing-dependent.",
        "Writing before expiry evaluation: would record clicks for expired links, violating the 'no click row on 410' criterion.",
        "Fire-and-forget logging to a file parsed later: adds a second source of truth and a file that could accumulate request metadata."
      ]
    },
    {
      "choice": "An ASGI middleware that runs before routing/body parsing and applies only when method == POST and path == /api/links. State is an in-process dict mapping a per-boot-salted SHA-256 of the client address to a deque of monotonic timestamps, pruned to the LINKS_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS rolling window; nothing is written to SQLite, disk, or logs. Client address is request.client.host, or the first X-Forwarded-For entry when LINKS_TRUST_FORWARDED_FOR is true. Retry-After = ceil(seconds until the oldest in-window timestamp ages out), minimum 1.",
      "decision": "Layer and key for rate limiting",
      "rationale": "Enforcing it before body parsing means a flood of oversized bodies is rejected cheaply, and the 429 arrives without touching the database, satisfying 'writes no row'. Hashing with a per-boot random salt means a memory dump or a crash report never yields a visitor IP, and the state vanishes on restart, satisfying 'no persisted client IP'. Because the limiter is keyed per address, a second client in the same window still gets 201.",
      "rejected": [
        "A SQLite-backed limiter table: would persist client addresses, directly violating the privacy constraint.",
        "Per-route dependency after body validation: a large invalid body would still be parsed before the limit applied, and 429 ordering versus 422 would become ambiguous.",
        "Global (non-keyed) limiting: one noisy client would lock out everyone.",
        "Redis token bucket: forbidden external datastore; unnecessary on a single node."
      ]
    },
    {
      "choice": "Pure parsing plus name resolution: urlsplit the URL, require scheme in {http, https} and a non-empty host; if the host parses as an IP literal (via ipaddress, after unwrapping brackets, IPv4-mapped ::ffff:a.b.c.d and IPv4-compatible forms, and after normalizing decimal/octal/hex IPv4 literals through the int/ipaddress constructors) check it directly; otherwise call socket.getaddrinfo for AF_UNSPEC and check every returned address. Reject if any address is loopback, private, link-local (including 169.254.169.254), unique-local, CGNAT 100.64.0.0/10, multicast, unspecified, or otherwise reserved. Unresolvable hosts are rejected with blocked_destination (fail closed). No HTTP client is ever constructed for a user-supplied URL, at creation or at redirect.",
