ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA
- POST /api/links with body {"url": "https://example.com/page?a=1"} returns 201 and a JSON body containing code, short_url, url (echoed unchanged including query string), created_at and expires_at; a corresponding row exists in the links table.
- POST /api/links with no expiry field stores expires_at equal to created_at plus 30 days (within 1 second tolerance) and returns that value in the response.
- POST /api/links with an ISO-8601 expires_at in the future stores and returns exactly that instant, normalized to UTC.
- POST /api/links with an expires_at that is in the past, equal to now, or not a valid ISO-8601 timestamp returns 400 with a JSON error body and writes no row.
- POST /api/links with url using a scheme other than http or https (e.g. ftp://host/f, file:///etc/passwd, javascript:alert(1), data:text/html,x) returns 400 with error code unsupported_scheme and writes no row.
- POST /api/links with a url whose host is a literal loopback address (127.0.0.1, 127.5.5.5, ::1, [::ffff:127.0.0.1]) returns 400 with error code blocked_destination and writes no row.
- POST /api/links with a url whose host is a literal private or reserved address (10.0.0.1, 172.16.0.5, 192.168.1.1, 169.254.169.254, 100.64.0.1, 0.0.0.0, fd00::1, fe80::1) returns 400 with error code blocked_destination and writes no row.
- POST /api/links with a url whose hostname resolves via DNS to a blocked address (test stubs the resolver to return 127.0.0.1) returns 400 with error code blocked_destination and writes no row.
- POST /api/links with a hostname resolving to multiple addresses where at least one is blocked returns 400 and writes no row.
- During creation and during redirect the service makes no outbound HTTP(S) request to the destination: a test that patches the HTTP client layer asserts zero calls while creation and redirect both succeed.
- POST /api/links with a missing url field, a non-string url, an empty string, or a url longer than the configured maximum (2048 characters) returns 422 or 400 with a JSON error body and writes no row.
- GET /{code} for an existing, unexpired link returns 307 with a Location header exactly equal to the stored URL and Cache-Control: no-store, and inserts exactly one click row.
- The click row written by GET /{code} contains the request timestamp, the Referer header value (null when absent) and the User-Agent header value (null when absent), and the clicks table schema contains no column holding a client IP address.
- GET /{code} for a code that does not exist returns 404 with a JSON error body and inserts no click row.
- GET /{code} for a link whose expires_at is in the past returns 410 with a JSON error body, sends no Location header, and inserts no click row.
- GET /api/links/{code}/stats for an existing link returns 200 with JSON containing code, url, created_at, expires_at, total_clicks and a clicks array whose entries each carry timestamp, referrer and user_agent; total_clicks equals the number of successful redirects served for that code.
- GET /api/links/{code}/stats for an expired but existing link returns 200 with the accumulated analytics (not 410).
- GET /api/links/{code}/stats for an unknown code returns 404 with a JSON error body.
- GET /api/links/{code}/stats supports limit and offset query parameters on the clicks array with a default limit of 50 and a maximum of 500; limit=1 on a link with 3 clicks returns 1 click entry and total_clicks 3.
- After the configured per-IP creation limit is reached (default 10 creations per 60-second rolling window), the next POST /api/links from the same client address returns 429 with a Retry-After header and writes no row, while a request from a different client address in the same window still returns 201.
- Rate limiting state holds no persisted client IP: no table or file written by the service contains a client IP address after the rate limiter has been exercised.
- GET /health returns 200 with {"status":"ok"} when the SQLite database answers a trivial query, and returns 503 with {"status":"degraded"} when the database is unavailable.
- Generating 1000 codes yields 1000 distinct values, each matching ^[A-Za-z0-9]{7}$, produced by a cryptographically secure random source; codes created in sequence are not ordered or incrementable (a test asserts consecutive codes differ in more than the last character).
- When code generation collides with an existing code, the service retries and still returns 201 with a unique code (test forces one collision via a seeded/stubbed generator).
- GET /{code} where code does not match the code character set and length returns 404 rather than a server error.
- All error responses across every endpoint are JSON objects with a stable shape {"error": {"code": <string>, "message": <string>}} and the documented HTTP status.

CONSTRAINTS
- Runtime: Python 3.11+ with FastAPI; all endpoints exchange JSON except the redirect response.
- Storage: SQLite single file, schema created/migrated at application startup; no external datastore.
- No authentication, user accounts, or API keys in this release; POST /api/links and the stats endpoint are publicly reachable.
- Abuse control must be per-client-IP rate limiting on POST /api/links only.
- Destination validation is a denylist: every public http/https URL is accepted; only non-http(s) schemes and destinations resolving to loopback, private (RFC1918), link-local (169.254.0.0/16, fe80::/10 — including 169.254.169.254), unique-local, CGNAT, multicast, unspecified and other reserved ranges are rejected.
- IPv4-mapped and IPv6-compatible representations of blocked ranges must be decoded and blocked (e.g. ::ffff:127.0.0.1, decimal/octal/hex IPv4 literals).
- The service must never issue an HTTP request to a user-supplied destination — no validation fetch, no HEAD probe, no metadata/preview retrieval, no redirect following.
- Visitor IP addresses must never be persisted to the database, to logs, or to analytics records.
- Short codes must be generated from a CSPRNG (secrets module), never from a sequence or database row id.
- Expired links must return 410 Gone from the redirect endpoint; expiry is evaluated against UTC at request time.
- All timestamps stored and returned in UTC ISO-8601 format.
- The redirect response must not be cached by intermediaries in a way that suppresses click recording (Cache-Control: no-store).
- The redirect endpoint GET /{code} must not shadow the /api/* or /health routes; API routes take precedence in routing order.

